=== S37: the faces of k agree on the known gap members ===

  member                                 ML  N     (s,k)  lock Ns<q<(N+1)s  q<=2max SBdepth
  Fan-Sun n=4  {3,8,11,19}             7/30  4  (7,2)              True     True    None
  Fan-Sun n=6  {5,6,11,17,23,28}       8/51  6  (8,3)              True     True    None
  Fan-Sun n=7  {1,3,4,5,7,13,18}       3/23  7  (3,2)  k<s<2k      True     True       1
  kps S35 n=7  {1,2,3,4,5,7,18}        3/23  7  (3,2)  k<s<2k      True     True       1

READING (corrected, honest): the lock Ns<q<(N+1)s and q<=2*max hold for EVERY member
(they are general).  BUT k<s<2k and a finite SB-depth-in-gap hold ONLY for genuine
FIRST-GAP members: the n=7 mediant 3/23 (depth 1, (s,k)=(3,2)).  The Fan-Sun n=4 (7/30)
and n=6 (8/51) are Kravitz counterexamples ABOVE the first gap (7/30>2/9, 8/51>2/13) --
so SB-depth=None and k<s<2k FAILS.  LESSON: a Kravitz counterexample is NOT automatically
a first-gap member; the first gap is far more restrictive (only mediant-type known:
n=7 -> 3/23 depth 1; n=6 -> 5/33 depth 2, kps S34).  On those, s,k,q,SB-depth,height
move together = ONE parameter, and it is SMALL.  kps S36 adds the mechanism face:
k = base defect order, depth-1 = mediant (s,k)=(3,2).

CRUX (all agree): bound this one parameter at N=12.  opus = finite residue system q=38
(the mediant 3/38); mac-mini = AP unique max-kissing (CKMRV LP-uniqueness) + Selberg spacing;
kps = the ladder Dx<D skip + a COMBINATORIAL defect-count bound (new lead).
